UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 2: Opinion and reasons', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Order a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nReorder: \"Please vote yes. Herbs flavour cooking. We should keep the class garden. Bees visit.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nOpinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes.\n\nCHECK\nPutting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite two persuasive paragraphs on different topics (90–110 words each) with opinion, two reasons with examples and a closing request. Label each part.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Order a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-03"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write two persuasive paragraphs on different topics (90–110 words each) with opinion, two reasons with examples and a closing request. Label each part. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes. Reasoning: I used opinion by attending to reason and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. Correction: Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes. Why: the correction restores accurate use of opinion.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating opinion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with opinion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes. This matches the lesson skill for opinion.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes.","Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Reorder: \"Please vote yes. Herbs flavour cooking. We should keep the class garden. Bees visit.\" Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Order a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request.","Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes.","Careful use of reason and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising opinion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named reason and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the reason and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising opinion. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries opinion.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show opinion and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses opinion from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes.","Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about opinion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise reason and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about opinion.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of opinion to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating opinion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes. Reasoning: I used opinion by attending to reason and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with opinion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. Correction: Opinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes. Why: the correction restores accurate use of opinion.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w16-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise opinion.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use opinion with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify reason and explain why it matters.","I can practise example independently on a new prompt.","I can notice order needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Order a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Order a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about opinion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"We should keep the class garden. Herbs flavour cooking. Bees visit the flowers. Please vote to continue watering rosters.\" Opinion, reasons, request.\n\nTRY FIRST\nReorder: \"Please vote yes. Herbs flavour cooking. We should keep the class garden. Bees visit.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nOpinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes.","explain":"Order a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Opinion placed before supporting reasons.","caption":"Order strengthens a persuasive paragraph.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Opinion first</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">keep the class garden</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Then reasons</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">herbs; bees</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"We should keep the class garden. Herbs flavour cooking. Bees visit the flowers. Please vote to continue watering roster Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"We should keep the class garden. Herbs flavour cooking. Bees visit the flowers. Please vote to continue watering roster Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about opinion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"We should keep the class garden. Herbs flavour cooking. Bees visit the flowers. Please vote to continue watering rosters.\" Opinion, reasons, request. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Closing request contrasted with unsupported ask.","caption":"Reasons should appear before the final push.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Close</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">vote to continue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Weak order</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">request with no reasons</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about opinion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPutting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. Here is a more productive model: \"We should keep the class garden. Herbs flavour cooking. Bees visit the flowers. Please vote to continue watering rosters.\" Opinion, reasons, request.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nOpinion first: We should keep the class garden. Then reasons. End with Please vote yes.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Putting the request first without reasons can weaken the argument. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about opinion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOrder a persuasive paragraph so the opinion comes early, reasons and examples follow, and the ending restates the request. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 2: Opinion and reasons","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Persuasion: opinions and reasons","week":16,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"We should keep the class garden. Herbs flavour cooking. Bees visit the flowers. Please vote to continue watering rosters.\" Opinion, reasons, request.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Opinion placed before supporting reasons.","caption":"Order strengthens a persuasive paragraph.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=